{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Investigate replacing the general dense matrix-power path with Kitamasa-style polynomial exponentiation, reducing the order-N recurrence in O(N² log H) rather than O(N³ log H). Derive the coefficient orientation from the source recurrence and verify the result against the base terms, including N=1 and modular arithmetic. Keep the measured short-distance route available for comparison; do not assume this approach wins without measurement.\",\"B\":\"Refine the strongest measured candidate: direct recurrence evaluation with a circular buffer for small distance d=H−N. Measure a general crossover across N and d, and choose dispatch based on robust evidence rather than a particular input. Preserve chronological window and reversed-coefficient alignment, verify modular bounds for block accumulation, and check boundary cases. The measured 4-way unroll was slower than the original short-distance branch, so retain it only if new measurements justify it.\",\"C\":\"Independently investigate Bostan–Mori for the recurrence’s generating function as an alternative general large-H path. Derive the numerator from the given initial terms and denominator coefficients, and validate index parity and coefficient extraction carefully, including N=1. Compare it with A’s approach and the existing matrix path; avoid duplicating A’s implementation. The measured OpenMP/transposed-matrix variants were slower than baseline, so do not assume parallelism or transposition helps on this four-core ARM64 target.\",\"G\":\"When candidate programs are available, integrate the best validated general path with B’s short-distance branch, then measure the integrated program: gains may not compose. Compare standalone and combined timings, retain a fallback if an optimization regresses, and require correctness for all valid inputs before selecting a candidate. Use the supplied correctness/measurement process without changing tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or unrelated source.\",\"analysis\":\"The source evaluates a weighted order-N recurrence using dense matrix exponentiation. The best measured result is B’s direct small-distance branch at 0.00624 s versus the 0.02140 s baseline; its four-way unroll measured 0.00772 s. The measured matrix-kernel candidates were slower than baseline (about 0.00941–0.01249 s), including the OpenMP variant. These are useful leads, not universal crossover evidence: pursue the demonstrated short-distance win and distinct recurrence-specific methods for large H, then judge combinations by integrated measurements while preserving correctness for every valid input.\"}"}}
